Change detection strategies:


- 🧠When we explained how Angular detects the changes in your application, we showed the tree of
    components and said that Angular starts by checking the root component, then its children, then its
    grand-children, until all components are checked. Then all the necessary DOM updates are applied
    in one batch.

    🔥But you may be wondering if it is a very good idea to check every component on every change. And
    you’re right, that’s often not really necessary.


- Angular offers two change detection strategies: 🔥Eager (which was previously named Default), and
    OnPush. 🔥Until Angular 21, the default strategy was Eager. 🔥Since Angular 22, the default
    strategy is OnPush.

    🔥🔥A component using the OnPush strategy will only be checked in four cases:
        • one of the inputs of the component changed (to be more accurate, when the reference of one of
            the inputs changes);
        • an event handler of the component was triggered;
        • the value of a signal read by the template of the component changed;
        • the component has been marked for check.


- ✅If you (and the Angular libraries you use) respect the rules described in this book regarding the
    use of signals, then the OnPush strategy (which is now the default one) shouldn’t cause any bug,
    and is the most performant choice. It avoids skipping a component (and its descendants) during the
    tree traversal if the state displayed by its template hasn’t changed.

    👏If all your components use OnPush and your application works fine, it’s a good sign that your code
    is already ready for zoneless synchronization.


- 🐛Let’s however demonstrate some of the gotchas of the OnPush strategy with small examples, so that
    you can identify the bugs in your code causing it to fail

    Imagine that we have 3 components working fine, but using the Eager strategy.
    👉A very simple Image:

        @Component({
            selector: 'ns-img',
            template: `
                <p>{{ check() }}</p>
                <img [src]="src()" />
            `,
            changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager
        })
        export class Image {
            readonly src = input.required<string>();
            protected check(): void {
                console.log('image component view checked');
            }
        }

    👉used in a Pony:

        @Component({
            selector: 'ns-pony',
            template: `
                <p>{{ check() }}</p>
                <ns-img [src]="ponyImageUrl()" />
            `,
            imports: [Image],
            changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager
        })
        export class Pony {
            readonly ponyModel = input.required<PonyModel>();
            protected readonly ponyImageUrl = computed(() => `images/pony-${this.ponyModel
                ().color}-running.gif`);

            protected check(): void {
                console.log('pony component view checked');
            }
        }

    👉used itself in a Race:

        @Component({
            selector: 'ns-race',
            template: `
                <h2>Race</h2>
                <p>{{ check() }}</p>
                @for (pony of ponies(); track pony.id) {
                    <div>
                        <ns-pony [ponyModel]="pony" />
                    </div>
                }
                <button (click)="changeColor()">Change color</button>
            `,
            imports: [Pony],
            changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager
        })
        export class Race {
            protected readonly ponies = signal<Array<PonyModel>>([
                { id: 1, color: 'green' },
                { id: 2, color: 'orange' }
            ]);
            protected readonly colors: Array<string> = ['green', 'orange', 'blue'];

            protected check(): void {
                console.log('race component view checked');
            }

            protected changeColor(): void {
                const ponies = this.ponies();
                ponies[0].color = this.randomColor();   // change color of the first pony
                this.ponies.set([...ponies]);
            }
        }
    
    💡The Race displays two ponies, and the user can change the color of the first one by clicking on
    the Change color button.

    🧠🔥With the Eager change detection strategy, every time that we have a change in the application,
    all 3 components are checked.

    We added a check() method in each component, called in each template: it allows us to track if the
    component is checked or not. And indeed in our example, we can see in our console:

        pony component view checked
        image component view checked
        pony component view checked
        image component view checked
        race component view checked
    
    (🧠we can see that twice actually, because we are in development mode - see the section about the
    production mode).


