Explanation:

    pony component view checked
    image component view checked
    pony component view checked
    image component view checked
    race component view checked
    
⁉️ We got this log but Why this specific order?

    ❌ Angular does not check all parents first and then all children.
    ✅ Instead, it performs a DEPTH-FIRST TRAVERSAL of the component tree.

    When the button is clicked Angular starts with the root component(Race). It starts evaluating the
    template from top to bottom.
    -> it executes check() and logs race component view checked
    -> Angular reaches the first Pony <ns-pony [ponyModel]="pony1" />
    -> Before it can continue with the rest of Race's template, it must check the child component.
        So angular enters Pony and inside Pony's template encounters <p>{{ check() }}</p>
        and logs pony component view checked
    -> Pony's template continues. Angular reaches <ns-img [src]="ponyImageUrl()" /> and enters Image
        and inside Image's template encounters <p>{{ check() }}</p>
        and logs image component view checked
    -> Image has no child components.
        Angular returns back to Pony.
        Pony is finished.
        Angular returns back to Race.
    -> Angular continues with Race in the same way for the second pony <ns-pony [ponyModel]="pony2" />
        we get the same log for the second pony and its image and at last we get the logs

        race component view checked
        pony component view checked
        image component view checked
        pony component view checked
        image component view checked

